b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/11935-3
01:40:59.387 --> 01:41:01.747
schedulers and things can do.

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/11943-0
01:41:01.947 --> 01:41:07.587
for the programmer without the programmer
having to explicitly change their code.

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/11986-0
01:41:09.347 --> 01:41:14.943
But there's limits on the speed up that
you can get from that kind of parallelism

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/11986-1
01:41:14.943 --> 01:41:19.651
without explicitly redesigning your
algorithms. And again, you know,

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/11986-2
01:41:19.651 --> 01:41:24.086
the only reason that we're still
continuing to see growth in the

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/11986-3
01:41:24.086 --> 01:41:27.907
transistor counts is because we went to
multiple cores.

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12031-0
01:41:28.147 --> 01:41:34.824
Instead of just a single core, now we,
you know, we've got multi-cores,

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12031-1
01:41:34.824 --> 01:41:42.429
many cores, GPUs with thousands of cores,
and then now we're starting to get this

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12031-2
01:41:42.429 --> 01:41:48.827
3D 2.5D, you know, packaging, layering,
and chiplets, and they're...

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12054-0
01:41:48.947 --> 01:41:54.429
taking all these new novel approaches to
get more transistor counts,

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12054-1
01:41:54.429 --> 01:42:00.227
but really the like the fundamental
density limitations are pretty flat.

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12057-0
01:42:01.587 --> 01:42:03.427
for the past five or 10 years.

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12092-0
01:42:05.707 --> 01:42:11.618
So all these reasons are basically
leading to one, you know,

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12092-1
01:42:11.618 --> 01:42:18.885
we can't just expect parallels like speed
ups and an automatic speed up of

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12092-2
01:42:18.885 --> 01:42:25.667
performance to keep happening without
active programmer intervention.

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12094-0
01:42:25.987 --> 01:42:26.627
and.

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12109-0
01:42:28.307 --> 01:42:34.116
Program or intervention needs to be
explicitly and keenly aware of this

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12109-1
01:42:34.116 --> 01:42:37.827
memory gap,
compute versus memory access gap.

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12164-0
01:42:40.867 --> 01:42:45.102
I mentioned a couple of these terms,
things like multi-core,

b706b327-f0cf-4124-aaba-f498e83a75d2/12164-1
01:42:45.102 --> 01:42:50.864
which is typically used for CPUs when we
